{"id":"pr-10-51d3068eb273-4b9f0e7dcbd1","problem":"yang-mills","policy":"56e3d0e86958d1f5f36976c8eecf234a3f8117557e7c5d6f6f0395d59be6234d","protocol_commit":"e72ff4a2b51e8cfa809865555116dfc3855e1e2e","worker_commit":"e72ff4a2b51e8cfa809865555116dfc3855e1e2e","contract":"0x983d27779dF7D495d4c881Ab0925B42A7bBf76d7","repository":"MuncleUscles/yang-mills","branch":"research","pr":10,"head":"51d3068eb273317a9abd1a536f7d86cc78b5aa47","base":"9004f95bc44e4a63215352a04e97344d42fb64aa","wallet":"0x8562799a4d8AC66F732fd47aF5c20018FF509f8F","claim_digest":"4b9f0e7dcbd17c61717af5249e51e4db7fb70e5c0510680723cbd203de329840","submission_hash":"464478319d58fbda29278bf0e612969bc71f1d44fce2719abb2d8996b5462620","github_author_id":2372232,"claim_comment_id":7230378688,"claim_comment_created_at":"2026-11-27T23:44:57Z","github_account_created_at":1347988519,"github_account_type":"User","statement_hash":"422061751f71bcfecb5178d1164eb2c189a6326649701b5f53fc1eb678ee9208","proposition_hash":"9ca557dfd63bdaaf411280e3802c9dbfe8ff79ad80ce968825875991e781928f","artifact_hash":"fe98bbfbfb97628232bb09c5964d9ee6fa045bd4f96501a460ab0583772ba5a2","bundle_hash":"fe98bbfbfb97628232bb09c5964d9ee6fa045bd4f96501a460ab0583772ba5a2","verification":"verified","claim":"On the periodic hypercubic lattice (ℤ/Lℤ)^d, for an arbitrary group G with link variables U : site → direction → G and gauge action U_{x,μ} ↦ g_x U_{x,μ} g_{x+μ}⁻¹, the Lean theorem Contribution.result proves: (1) the plaquette holonomy transforms by conjugation at its base point, so every plaquette action ∑_x ∑_{μ<ν} f(U_p) with a class function f is exactly gauge invariant; (2) for any unitary representation ρ : G → M_n(ℂ) (unitary-valued and multiplicative, predicate IsUnitaryRep), the Wilson action S_ρ(U) = ∑_x ∑_{μ<ν} (n − Re tr ρ(U_p)) is gauge invariant and nonnegative, and S_ρ(U) = 0 iff ρ(U_p) = 1 for every plaquette (all orientations); if ρ is injective, S_ρ(U) = 0 iff U is flat (every plaquette holonomy is 1); (3) for unitary V, 2(n − Re tr V) = ∑_{i,j} |(V − 1)_{ij}|², i.e. the Wilson weight is half the squared Frobenius distance to the identity. Why it matters: these are the exact classical inputs to the lattice route to the mass gap (Wilson 1974; Osterwalder–Seiler strong coupling; Chatterjee's probabilistic program). The identity in (3) gives the coercivity ‖V−1‖² ≤ 2·cost used in small-field / weak-coupling analysis, and (2) identifies the minimizers of the Wilson action — the classical vacua around which any weak-coupling or continuum-limit argument must expand — for every compact gauge group presented through a faithful unitary representation (SU(n), SO(n), Sp(n), and by Peter–Weyl every compact Lie group). The theorem is a reusable, dependency-free bundle in the track's pinned Lean 4.36 / Mathlib environment.","kind":"infrastructure","research_direction":"Lattice regularization route: formal classical foundations of Wilson lattice Yang–Mills for arbitrary compact gauge groups","provenance":{"mode":"extension","sources":[{"reference":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.10.2445","title":"Confinement of quarks","authors":["Kenneth G. Wilson"],"relationship":"Source of the Wilson lattice action and its gauge invariance; the formalized parts (1)–(2) restate this classical setup.","priority_evidence":"Published in Physical Review D 10, 2445 (1974); priority for the lattice action and its gauge invariance belongs to Wilson."},{"reference":"https://github.com/mrdouglasny/lgt","title":"lgt — Lattice Gauge Theory in Lean 4","authors":["Michael R. Douglas"],"relationship":"Prior independent Lean formalization of U(n) Wilson lattice gauge theory including gauge invariance; this bundle overlaps it in part and extends it with the vacuum characterization, Frobenius identity and representation-level statement.","priority_evidence":"Public Apache-2.0 repository; inspected commit b8793ccf6a51e00e9e2b1685ba191b8626e37137 dated 2026-07-21, which predates this submission."}],"discovery_basis":"Prior-art search on 2026-11-27: web searches for Lean formalizations of lattice gauge theory and the Wilson action (found lgt and several unreviewed GitHub projects claiming lattice Yang–Mills formalizations, which were not audited), plus a grep of lgt at commit b8793ccf for zero-set, flatness, Frobenius and faithfulness statements. No search of unpublished or non-indexed work. The Frobenius identity and the zero-set characterization are elementary and likely appear informally in textbooks; no priority is claimed for the mathematics, only for this formal bundle."},"dependencies":[],"verified_dependencies":[],"formal_statement":"theorem : (app (app And (forall (Sort (succ 0)) (forall (app Group.{0} #0) (forall Nat (forall Nat (forall (app (app (app NeZero.{0} Nat) (app (app MulZeroClass.toZero.{0} Nat) Nat.instMulZeroClass)) #0) (forall (forall #4 Real) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.IsClassFunction #5) #4) #0) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.GaugeTransform #6) #4) #3) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.LinkConfig #7) #5) #4) (app (app (app Eq.{(succ 0)} Real) (app (app (app (app (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.latticeAction #8) #7) #6) #5) #4) #3) (app (app (app (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.gaugeAct #8) #7) #6) #5) #1) #0))) (app (app (app (app (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.latticeAction #8) #7) #6) #5) #4) #3) #0)))))))))))) (app (app And (forall (Sort (succ 0)) (forall (app Group.{0} #0) (forall Nat (forall Nat (forall Nat (forall (app (app (app NeZero.{0} Nat) (app (app MulZeroClass.toZero.{0} Nat) Nat.instMulZeroClass)) #0) (forall (forall #5 (app (app (app Matrix.{0,0,0} (app Fin #4)) (app Fin #4)) Complex)) (forall (app (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.IsUnitaryRep #4) #6) #5) #0) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.LinkConfig #7) #4) #3) (app (app And (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.GaugeTransform #8) #5) #4) (app (app (app Eq.{(succ 0)} Real) (app (app (app (app (app (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.wilsonAction #7) #9) #8) #